---
description: Design rules for maintainable Unity code - simplicity, reuse, ownership, dependencies, and cohesion
globs: **/Assets/**/*
alwaysApply: false
---

# Simplicity
- Build the smallest design that meets the current requirement. Add an interface, base class, or generic only when a second real use exists.
- Comments state a constraint or reason the code cannot show; do not narrate what the code does.

# Reuse
- Before adding a manager, utility, or base class, search `Assets/Scripts/` and [`unity/starter-infrastructure.mdc`](unity/starter-infrastructure.mdc). Extend the existing type (new enum entry, subclass, config field) instead of writing a parallel one.

# Ownership
- Each piece of state or config has exactly one owner. Other classes read it from that owner; they do not keep a copy that can drift.
- Keep flow orchestration (coordinators, example-style scene scripts) separate from reusable logic, so the logic works in another flow unchanged.

# Dependencies
- Pass each class only what it uses, via `Initialize(...)`. Do not pass a container or manager just so the callee can reach one member of it.
- Reusable components receive their dependencies from the parent; reserve `Singleton.Instance` lookups for app-wide services.

```csharp
// ❌ Player needs one engine but receives the whole scene controller
player.Initialize(gameController);

// ✅ Pass exactly what it uses
player.Initialize(playerIndex, detectionEngine, playerPhotoManager);
```

# Cohesion
- Name a class after its single responsibility. Do not create catch-all `Helper`, `Common`, or `GameManager` classes that collect unrelated features.
- Split a class when it has unrelated reasons to change, not only when it reaches the size limit in [`unity/code-editing.mdc`](unity/code-editing.mdc).

# Null Handling
- Do not add defensive null checks for references that must exist; let the error surface. See "Null Handling" in [`unity/code-editing.mdc`](unity/code-editing.mdc).
